  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nToday: Breezy with Scattered Showers\n\nA positively tilted H500 trough will continue to move east and dig \nsouth across the central CONUS throughout the day today. At the \nsurface, a cold front has already moved south of the region and \nnorthwest to north wind will prevail in its wake. Sufficient \nmoisture should remain in place to generate some scattered showers \nthis morning. The shower activity should wane by mid-day as the \nsynoptic lift exits off to the east. What will be more noticeable, \nwill be the northwest to north wind for the first half of the day. \nThe wind will continue to increase through the night and should peak \nafter sunrise through mid-day. The stronger wind will be aided in \npart by a tightening surface pressure gradient combined with a LLJ \nat H850 upwards of 40-45 kts. The newest NBM data continues to paint \nthe higher probabilities for gusts of 45 mph or higher (40-60%) \nacross northeastern Iowa into southwestern Minnesota. The wind will \nfinally decrease this afternoon but moreso this evening into the \novernight hours.\n\nTonight into Saturday: Freeze Very Likely Area Wide.\n\nColder air will continue to stream southeast over the Midwest into \nthe Great Lakes region today into Saturday morning. Drier mid-level \nair moves in which will aid in clearing out the clouds and clear \nskies are expected throughout the night. The clearing skies combined \nwith cooler airmass and light winds will lead to really good \nradiational cooling tonight. Overnight lows should be able to easily \ndrop down into the mid to low 30s, with some locations across west \ncentral Wisconsin potentially dropping into the mid to lower 20s. \nThe entire area has at least a 70% chance to drop down to freezing \nand at least a 30% chance to drop down to 28 degrees, or the hard \nfreeze threshold. The Freeze Watch has been converted to a Freeze \nWarning as confidence in freezing to sub-freezing temperatures \ncontinues to increase.\n\nSunday into Next Week: Mostly Dry and Warmer Conditions\n\nSynoptic scale ridging returns to the central and eastern CONUS late \nthis weekend into early next week. This pattern will be quite \nfavorable for temperatures to quickly rise back up to near to above \naverage as early as Sunday. The afternoon high temperatures should \nrise by a few degrees each day through the middle of next week. This \npattern will not be favorable for precipitation chances so dry \nconditions are anticipated to start the next work week. That may \nchange as we get into the later half of next week as a trough digs \nacross the western CONUS and should eventually move east, but this \nis about a week out so confidence in any one scenario panning out is \nvery low at this point in time.",
